  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>From quai Laubeuf in Cannes, several shuttle boat companies reach the Lérins Islands archipelago in a few minutes: Sainte-Marguerite, wooded, classified as a biological reserve in 2006 and managed by the French forestry office (ONF), and Saint-Honorat, home to a Cistercian abbey.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Both shuttle companies say so on their websites. <strong>Planaria</strong> (to Saint-Honorat) writes on its ticket page: "Dogs are accepted provided they are kept on a leash." <strong>Trans Côte d'Azur</strong> (to Sainte-Marguerite) states: "Our friends the dogs are welcome on board our boats and on Sainte-Marguerite island (on a leash)." The Cannes tourist office press kit adds, for Sainte-Marguerite, that dogs must be kept on a leash, bicycles and smoking are forbidden and there are no bins on the island.</p>
      <p>For <strong>Saint-Honorat</strong>, the official source read only covers the crossing: Planaria does not state the rule for dogs on the island itself. Neither company publishes a fare for the dog: ask the operator when booking.</p>
      <p>Practicalities: Planaria publishes shuttles every day from 4 April to 1 November 2026, with departures from Cannes between 9am and 6pm (check the following period's times with the company); the return fare is €19.50 for an adult and €12 for a child aged 4 to 15, and its ticket office is at quai des îles, at the end of the Laubeuf car park. Trans Côte d'Azur serves Sainte-Marguerite all year, with departures from 7:30am to 4:30pm (except Sundays from 1 November to 1 April) and returns from 7:45am to 6pm; the return fare is €19 for an adult and €13.50 for a child aged 5 to 12, plus a €1.91 port fee. The Laubeuf car park is closed: the Pantiero, Suquet Forville and Palais des festivals car parks are listed nearby.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: there are no bins on Sainte-Marguerite, so bring bags to take droppings and waste back with you.</div>

      <h2>On Sainte-Marguerite island</h2>
      <p>The island has a botanical trail, a bird reserve, aquariums, and the Fort Royal with the Man in the Iron Mask museum. Two restaurants near the fort open at lunchtime from April to October. Drones, bicycles and scooters are forbidden, as is spear fishing.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Sainte-Marguerite offers long wooded walks, while Saint-Honorat suits a shorter visit around the abbey.</p>
